                <pb facs="0178" n="144" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg144"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">795.00/4–1552</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum on the Events of April 14, by the Deputy
                        Assistant Secretary of State for Far Eastern Affairs (Johnson)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-04-15">April 15, 1952</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <label>Subject:</label>
                    <item>Korean Armistice Negotiations</item>
                </list>
                <p>General Ridgway’s C 66832<anchor n="1" xml:id="d87-f1"/> was discussed the
                    morning of April 14 with the Secretary, the Under Secretary, Mr. Bohlen, Mr.
                    Nitze, Mr. Allison and Mr. Johnson. As a result of that meeting, a meeting with
                    the JCS was requested and held early in the afternoon of April 14,<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d87-f2"/> at which General Bradley, General Collins, General
                    Vandenberg and Admiral Fechteler were present for the JCS and Mr. Bohlen, Mr.
                    Nitze, Mr. Allison and Mr. Johnson for State. There was a general discussion of
                    problems raised by the fact that the screening thus far indicated a far higher
                    portion of prisoners of war who would violently object to repatriation than had
                    been anticipated. A radio to General Ridgway on the subject was discussed after
                    the meeting and drafted by Mr. Johnson and General Bolte. The radio, together
                    with some subsequent minor changes suggested by State, was approved by the JCS
                    and subsequently by the President with whom it was discussed by General Bradley
                    and <pb facs="0179" n="145" xml:id="pg145"/> the Secretary of State. It was
                    despatched the same afternoon as JCS 906314.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d87-f3"/></p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
